To begin solving this problem, first, we need to expand the left-hand side of the equation:


\begin{align*}
&amp;\textbf{expand}\left(\frac{3}{1}\right)\cdot\left(5^{2}-4\right)-\left(25-\left(10a+\frac{a^{2}}{-10}\right)
\end{align*}

This expanded form is equivalent to the original equation, so we can set this equal sign to something new:


\begin{align*}
&amp;\textbf{simplify}\left(\frac{3}{1}\right)\cdot\left(5^{2}-4\right)-\left(25-\left(10a+\frac{a^{2}}{-10}\right)
\end{align*}

Now we can substitute the right-hand side of this equation to simplify the left:


\begin{align*}
&amp;\textbf{substitute}\left(25-\left(10a+\frac{a^{2}}{-10}\right)\right)\cdot\left(\frac{3}{1}\right)\cdot\left(5^{2}-4\right)-\left(25\right.
\end{align*}

Finally, we can combine like terms on both sides of the equation:


\begin{align*}
&amp;\textbf{combine}\left(\frac{3}{1}\right)\cdot\left(5^{2}-4\right)-\left(25\right.
\end{align*}


The answer is \boxed{\boxed{25}}.

